exiftool is the absolute gold standard in digital forensics, OSINT (Open Source Intelligence), and incident response for
analyzing images, audio, video, and PDF files.

Unlike basic file property viewers, exiftool can read thousands of different metadata tags, including EXIF, IPTC, XMP,
GPS, and manufacturer-specific "MakerNotes" (from camera brands like Canon, Nikon, Sony).In a digital investigation, 
it is crucial because metadata acts as a digital fingerprint, often revealing things the suspect thought they had 
deleted or hidden.

Key Forensic Data exiftool Can Extract

Geolocation Data (GPS): Extracts exact latitude and longitude coordinates embedded by smartphones or cameras. This allows 
investigators to pin down the physical location where a photo was taken.

Camera & Device Details: Identifies the specific phone model, camera body, lens serial number, and software version used
to create or edit the file.

Timestamps & Execution History: Shows creation dates, modification dates, and sometimes even the local timezone. This is
essential for building a chronological timeline of events.Software Artifacts: Reveals if an image was manipulated in 
Photoshop or GIMP, as editing software always leaves trace metadata or software tags behind.

Practical exiftool Cheat Sheet :

1. Read All Metadata (The Baseline Test)To view every piece of metadata hidden inside a file:
    
        exiftool suspicious_image.jpg

2. Extract Specific Tags (e.g., GPS and Time)If a file has too much data and you only want to see when it was taken and
where:      

        exiftool -DateTimeOriginal -GPSPosition photo.jpg

3. Extract GPS Coordinates as a Google Maps LinkYou can format the output directly into a URL to instantly see the
location on a map:

        exiftool -c "%.6f" -p "https://google.com\$gpslatitude,\$gpslongitude" photo.jpg

4. Extract Hidden Preview Images or ThumbnailsCameras and smartphones often embed a smaller "thumbnail" image inside the 
metadata of the main file. Even if a suspect crops out a face or a piece of evidence from the main photo, the hidden 
thumbnail might still show the original, unedited image.

        exiftool -ThumbnailImage -b photo.jpg > extracted_thumbnail.jpg

5. Wipe Metadata (Anti-Forensics)If you want to clear all metadata from a file before sharing it to protect your own 
privacy:

        exiftool -all= photo.jpg
